// The three shift countdown, which decides which errors a trace does not carry. After a syntax error the parser is out
// of step with the input, so the errors it runs into next are most likely consequences of the one already reported.
// Suppressing them until three tokens of the input have been shifted again is what keeps a single mistake from producing
// an avalanche of messages. It is the rule of yacc and of GNU Bison, and every generated driver has to suppress exactly
// as much as every other.
//
// The input contains both halves of that rule. The second broken statement follows the first one immediately, so its
// error falls inside the countdown and no error is reported for it at all - the recovery still happens, which is why the
// trace has a round of recovery with no error in front of it. The last broken statement comes after a whole statement
// was parsed successfully, so the countdown has run out and its error is reported again.
//
// The second recovery is also the one which pops nothing: the parser is already in a state which allows the error
// symbol, so the round reports POP 0. That event is emitted even though it moves nothing, so that a round of recovery is
// the same number of events wherever it happens.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    PLUS:      "+";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    statements
        : statements statement
        | statement
        ;

    statement
        : expression ";"
        | @error ";"
        ;

    expression
        : NUMBER
        | expression "+" NUMBER
        ;
}
